# Copy to .env and fill in. The daemon refuses to start without a credential.
#   REEMOAT_TOKEN=$(openssl rand -hex 16)
REEMOAT_TOKEN=

# Which file the supervisor sources, and the one variable that cannot live in it.
#
# Nothing in this repository reads a `.env` on its own — there is no dotenv
# dependency and no `--env-file` in `src/` or `scripts/`. `deploy/run-daemon.sh`
# is what `.`-sources a file, and REEMOAT_ENV_FILE is how it is told which one
# (default: ~/.reemoat/daemon.env). Setting it *here* is circular, so it belongs
# in the supervisor's own environment — and a host installed with an override
# needs it in `deploy/deploy.sh`'s environment too, which is the same fact stated
# in .claude/rules/deployment.md.

# How the daemon decides who is asking.
#   shared_secret (default) — one secret, all scopes, no expiry, no control
#                             plane anywhere. The single-machine case, and the
#                             zero-configuration way to try this: `pnpm client`
#                             and `pnpm harness` both work against it with nothing
#                             else set up.
#   signed                  — only tokens signed by the control plane this
#                             machine enrolled with. REEMOAT_TOKEN is ignored.
#   both                    — accepts either, chosen by shape.
#
# `both` is a migration and break-glass mode, not a destination: the shared
# secret bypasses every grant and every scope the control plane issued. It
# exists because enrolling a daemon you reach over the network is exactly the
# moment you can lock yourself out of it.
# REEMOAT_AUTH=shared_secret

# Where to enroll, and the single-use code to do it with. Needed once, ever.
# The daemon exchanges the code for its machine id and the public keys that
# verify tokens addressed to it, writes both to REEMOAT_DB, and never contacts
# the control plane again — not to refresh a key, not to check a revocation
# list. That is why a control-plane outage cannot touch a running session, and
# why a daemon with no control plane at all still works.
#
# Nothing the daemon caches expires. Only the *user's* token does, and the
# client renews that, not the daemon.
#
# Restarting with the same code makes no network call; restarting with a new one
# re-enrolls, which is also how a control-plane signing key is rotated. Codes
# are single-use and expire after an hour.
# REEMOAT_CONTROL_PLANE=https://control-plane.example
# REEMOAT_ENROLL_CODE=ec_…

# **Every client reaches this daemon through the control plane's relay, and there
# is no other way in.** A daemon that was handed a relay at enrollment dials out
# over WSS and holds one connection; browsers and `pnpm client` reach it through
# that. There is nothing to switch on, and `REEMOAT_RELAY=0` is gone: opting out
# of the relay is opting out of being reachable, which is not a configuration.
#
# The cost, stated where it will be read: this daemon holds a permanent outbound
# connection to its control plane, and an outage there costs *all* reachability.
# What it does not cost is anything running — verification is entirely local, the
# tunnel is never *asked* anything, and a session in flight neither notices nor
# stops. That is the half the whole design rests on and it is untouched.
#
# What it buys, and what a plain tunnel cannot: the relay checks the caller's
# signed token **and their grant** before a byte enters the tunnel, against live
# rows. So revoking a grant takes effect on the next request rather than within a
# token lifetime.
#
# REEMOAT_RELAY is still *read*, and for one reason: to warn when it is set. A
# value left behind in an env file after the switch disappeared would otherwise
# look like it was still deciding something.
# REEMOAT_RELAY=   # deprecated, ignored

# Where to bind. Loopback, and this is the line that makes the paragraph above
# true rather than merely intended.
#
# It used to be 0.0.0.0 so a Tailnet address worked without extra configuration,
# back when a browser reached a daemon directly. Nothing outside addresses this
# listener now: the relay routes by the verified `aud` claim and splices each
# CONNECT to a fresh loopback connection here.
#
# Clearing a machine's address in the registry was never the lever on its own —
# the daemon went on listening on every interface regardless, and a client that
# had already memoised a direct route went on using it. This is.
#
# The port stays known and stays 7887, because the things that *do* address it
# are on this machine: `pnpm client` under the shared secret, and the deploy
# script's /health probe. REEMOAT_PORT=0 works for a daemon that is only ever
# served by the relay.
REEMOAT_HOST=127.0.0.1
REEMOAT_PORT=7887

# Where the directory picker starts. PATH-style, like the shell's.
#
# **A narrowing of the listing, and not a boundary.** The agent runs as the user
# this daemon runs as, so it can already reach anything that user can — a narrower
# root is about not scrolling past `/Library` on a phone, not about safety. `cwd`
# is deliberately *not* confined to it, so a repository you keep in /opt or on an
# external volume is still somewhere a session can start.
#
# Unset, the picker starts at this user's home.
# REEMOAT_ROOTS=

# REEMOAT_USER_ROOT is deprecated and gets its own warning at startup rather than
# being ignored quietly, because its value names a directory that holds real
# sessions and worktrees written by a daemon that no longer reads there. Agents
# run as this user, on this filesystem; there is no per-user tree any more, and
# REEMOAT_ROOTS above is what chooses where browsing starts.
# REEMOAT_USER_ROOT=   # deprecated, ignored

# The container settings are deprecated too, and warned about together for the
# same reason: agents no longer run in containers at all, so a leftover memory
# or cpu bound is not a bound that got smaller — it is one that stopped existing.
#   REEMOAT_CONTAINER_IMAGE, REEMOAT_CONTAINER_HOME, REEMOAT_CONTAINER_USER,
#   REEMOAT_CONTAINER_MEMORY, REEMOAT_CONTAINER_CPUS, REEMOAT_CONTAINER_PIDS,
#   REEMOAT_MAX_CONTAINERS

# Where durable state lives: the event log and session metadata, in one SQLite
# file. Survives a restart — a session that was running when the daemon died
# comes back as `interrupted` with its transcript and sequence numbers intact,
# and can be resumed. The file holds full agent transcripts and every file diff,
# so it is created 0600 inside a 0700 directory.
# Default: ~/.reemoat/reemoat.db   Use :memory: for the old, non-durable behaviour.
# REEMOAT_DB=

# How long a session stays on disk, and how many are kept at all. Sessions past
# either bound are pruned at startup and disappear from GET /sessions.
# REEMOAT_SESSION_TTL_DAYS=7
# REEMOAT_MAX_SESSIONS=200

# On startup, SIGKILL agent process groups the previous daemon left behind. Only
# ever attempted for sessions created since the last boot — pids are recycled and
# a reboot resets them. Set to 0 to leave orphans alone.
# REEMOAT_REAP_ORPHANS=1

# On startup, put a fresh agent back on every session this daemon itself ended —
# a restart, a deploy, a crash — so a conversation survives being redeployed and
# you go on talking to it in the same context. A session you stopped by hand is
# never resumed; that is the one thing "stopped" means. The same switch also
# governs the transparent resume when you send a message to an interrupted
# session, because an operator who turned this off to stop agents spawning at
# boot did not mean "spawn them on a keystroke instead".
#
# The interrupted *turn* is not re-run. The agent comes back with the whole
# conversation and idle, not carrying on where it was cut off.
# REEMOAT_AUTO_RESUME=1

# Whether an agent may stop and ask you a question instead of guessing.
#
# This withdraws a *tool*, not a piece of UI. Measured against claude-agent-acp
# 0.63.0: the adapter puts "AskUserQuestion" into disallowedTools unless the
# client declares clientCapabilities.elicitation.form, so with this off the model
# never sees the tool and cannot ask at all. It also governs questions forwarded
# from MCP servers and the CLI's model-fallback consent prompt.
#
# codex-acp 1.1.9 reads the same capability with the same expression and answers
# its own ask-the-user tool with empty answers when it is absent, so this switch
# means the same thing there. Note codex keeps that tool behind its own
# "default_mode_request_user_input" feature flag, which ships off — so turning
# this on is necessary for a codex question and not sufficient.
#
# Why you might turn it off: a question parks the turn on a human until somebody
# answers or the turn is swept. On a machine nobody is watching — a scheduled
# session, a driver — that turns "finishes" into "waits". Same reasoning as the
# switch above, and the same shape of regret if you get it wrong.
# REEMOAT_ELICITATION=1

# Whether a claude session nobody has decided about asks for ultracode: highest
# effort, and every turn planned as a workflow of subagents. It is claude's own
# session setting, not an ACP option, so it is asked for at the door — which is why
# changing it on a live session restarts the agent (the conversation is kept, the
# same way a deploy keeps it) and why this switch is what makes that restart rare.
#
# Per session it is a row in the effort menu, and a choice made there outranks this
# in both directions and for ever. Only claude has it; kimi and codex ignore it. It
# also needs a model that supports the highest effort level — the row is absent on
# one that does not, which is the agent's own answer rather than a list of ours.
# REEMOAT_CLAUDE_ULTRACODE=1

# Each session gets its own git worktree, so two agents on one repo do not fight
# over one index and one HEAD — which is most of what makes running several at
# once bearable. A worktree branches from a commit, so uncommitted work in the
# main checkout is NOT in it; the session's log carries a warning saying so.
# Worktrees are never removed automatically.
#   auto (default) — make one whenever the cwd is a git repo with a commit
#   require        — refuse to create a session that cannot have one
#   never          — off daemon-wide
# REEMOAT_WORKTREE=auto
# Where they live. Somewhere out of the way, which is the whole requirement:
#   ~/.reemoat/worktrees/<repo>/<session-id>
# REEMOAT_WORKTREE_ROOT=

# Files attached to a prompt from the web UI, staged here before the agent is
# told about them:
#   ~/.reemoat/uploads/<session-id>/<upload-id>/<name>
# Beside the worktrees rather than inside one, on purpose: a worktree can be
# removed with `DELETE /sessions/:id/workspace` while staged input has to outlive
# that, and for a session running in a plain directory the workspace root IS your
# own checkout — attachments would land in your repository and show up as
# untracked files. The daemon refuses to start if this and REEMOAT_WORKTREE_ROOT
# contain one another, because each of the two `rm` paths is guarded by a
# containment check against its own root and nesting them makes both meaningless.
# REEMOAT_UPLOAD_ROOT=
# Branch names are <prefix>/<session-id>, so `git branch --list "reemoat/*"` is
# the whole clean-up story.
# REEMOAT_BRANCH_PREFIX=reemoat

# Changes-API bounds. Tunable for the same reason as the log bounds below: so the
# truncation path can be exercised without manufacturing 2000 changed files.
# REEMOAT_CHANGES_MAX_FILES=2000
# REEMOAT_DIFF_MAX_BYTES=524288

# Per-session event log bounds. Optional, and there is NO DEFAULT: a session's
# log is never truncated.
#
# It used to default to 5000 events / 8 MiB, evicting a *prefix* — which meant a
# conversation you were still working in could lose its beginning, permanently,
# with the client unable to tell that from a conversation that started there.
# Measured on a real session: `dropped: 6144`, oldest surviving event the two
# characters " for". There is no number that makes that acceptable, because the
# part a prefix eviction takes first is the part that says what the work is.
#
# Setting these turns eviction back on for this daemon, which is worth having for
# a machine with a small disk and for exercising the path — but understand that
# it destroys the oldest part of long conversations.
#
# They no longer need to stay below the WS outbound queue bound: `attach` caps its
# own replay (ATTACH_REPLAY_MAX in src/server.ts) and tells the client to page the
# rest over HTTP, so an unbounded log no longer collapses a `since=0` attach.
#
# What still bounds the database is whole sessions rather than parts of one —
# 7 days / 200 sessions, pruned at startup.
# REEMOAT_LOG_EVENTS=5000
# REEMOAT_LOG_BYTES=8388608

# How many sessions may run at once here, and how fast new ones may be made.
#
# Backstops rather than opinions, and they exist because `POST /sessions` had no
# bound of any kind: it ran a real `git worktree add` and spawned an agent, once
# per request, for ever. The only thing that counted sessions was the startup
# prune — and that counts in order to *delete*, keeping the newest 200 rows and
# taking every other transcript with it.
#
# That was harmless while a daemon served one person. A grant is what makes it
# matter: anybody you share a machine with can create sessions on it, and a loop
# fills the newest-200 with empty rows, so the next restart deletes your
# conversations. The daemon deliberately does not know *who* is asking, so the
# bound is on the rate rather than on the person — which is the right shape
# anyway, since what makes the prune destructive is how fast rows appear.
#
# The live ceiling counts sessions that hold, or are entitled to, an agent;
# stopping one frees a slot. Resuming is deliberately outside it, so a daemon
# coming back from a deploy always restores the work it was holding.
#
# The burst is spent by any create that gets past the ceiling, including one that
# then fails — the alternative is doing the expensive part before deciding
# whether to refuse. Both are in memory, so a restart clears them.
# REEMOAT_MAX_LIVE_SESSIONS=64
# REEMOAT_SESSION_CREATE_BURST=16
# REEMOAT_SESSION_CREATE_REFILL_MS=120000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read by `pnpm client`, not by the daemon.
#
# They are here because this is the only file that documents an environment for
# this repository, and the alternative was what happened: two of them appeared in
# no document at all and the other two only in CLAUDE.md. A daemon that is handed
# any of these ignores every one of them, which is why they sit in a section of
# their own rather than beside the settings above.
#
# `pnpm client --help` prints all four with their live values, so the check for
# "did this get through" is that command rather than this file.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# One daemon on this machine, addressed directly, with the shared secret above.
# Default: http://127.0.0.1:7887 — the same port REEMOAT_PORT defaults to, since
# what addresses that listener is on this machine by construction.
#
# It is also a switch and not only an address: with REEMOAT_URL set explicitly the
# client will not re-resolve a route after a failure, because there is nothing to
# re-resolve and retrying only doubles the time it takes to report the same error.
# REEMOAT_URL=http://127.0.0.1:7887

# Or, against a control plane. **All three, or none** — the client is in
# control-plane mode only when every one of them is non-empty, and then it asks
# for a short-lived token for one machine, learns that machine's URL from the same
# answer, and renews before it expires, including across an expiry mid-stream.
# REEMOAT_TOKEN and REEMOAT_URL are unused in that mode.
#
# The key is one of the account's own API keys (`rk_…`, minted in the web UI or
# with `cpctl`), and it never leaves this machine: what reaches the daemon is the
# token the control plane signs for it. The machine is whatever you call it —
# your own label for it, its `m_…` id, or the row's real name, resolved in that
# order and never against somebody else's label.
# REEMOAT_CP_URL=https://control-plane.example
# REEMOAT_CP_KEY=rk_…
# REEMOAT_MACHINE=laptop

# REEMOAT_ROUTE is deprecated and warned about, along with the choice it pinned:
# it selected between a direct URL and the relay, which was a real decision while
# both existed. Against a control plane there is one path now — the tunnel the
# daemon dialled out — and without one there is REEMOAT_URL on loopback.
# REEMOAT_ROUTE=   # deprecated, ignored
